window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-dac213f38c294e77"]={"key":"ny_main-dac213f38c294e77","group":"ny_main","uid":"dac213f38c294e77","rank":90,"title":"meadowgate cross jurisdiction safeguarding response","occupation":"child_family_school_social_workers","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You are Petra Yamamoto, the child and family social worker supporting Meadowgate Cooperative’s seasonal-worker accommodation in England and Scotland. Camila Ellingsen at Peatmoor Networks needs our oversight response by 16:00 on Friday 4 April 2025 after Peatmoor reported that safeguarding referrals increased between the 2023–24 and 2024–25 financial years. No referral log, case records, occupancy figures, counts, or underlying analysis came with that report. Ingrid Egwuatu is waiting to decide whether enhanced family-support action is required. Peatmoor’s correspondence routinely uses “referral” in two ways: a concern recorded within Meadowgate and a matter passed to statutory services. The response must make the period comparison intelligible despite that ambiguity. Camila also wants location-level information sufficient to test how Meadowgate responded, but several accommodation locations housed only one family, so even unnamed detail may identify the people involved. Prepare `meadowgate_safeguarding_response.docx` for Camila and Ingrid, applying safeguarding law and practice as at 31 March 2025. Give a defensible comparison of the two periods, make clear what can and cannot be concluded, address the relevant boundaries in England and Scotland, and set out the safeguarding and reporting actions that should follow. A full case assessment is not possible this week, so state and justify a professionally safe fallback. Ingrid has authorized you either to retain routine monitoring or to change the matter to interim enhanced review in this response; record which status takes effect, its effective date, its owner, and its review point. Give Ingrid one clear recommendation on the longer-term enhanced family-support decision, with concrete ownership and timing for the work Peatmoor will expect to see. Do not invent counts, rates, outcomes, incidents, or details about any child or family, and do not present the reported increase as a completed assessment. Keep the response to no more than six pages, including any tables or appendices. Write the file into the top level of the working directory using exactly the filename `meadowgate_safeguarding_response.docx`.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `meadowgate_safeguarding_response.docx` — An oversight-ready safeguarding case response comparing the two reported periods, applying the England and Scotland boundaries, recording the authorized interim status, and supporting the enhanced family-support decision. (Place the file at the top level of the working directory.; Use exactly the filename meadowgate_safeguarding_response.docx.; Maximum six rendered pages, including tables and appendices.; Apply law and practice as at 31 March 2025.; Do not invent quantitative results, case outcomes, incidents, or identifying details.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/meadowgate_safeguarding_response.docx","bytes":44575,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/1a18ed9750e0c2d93420c8c3b756a689f6bd5ead0dcb431d42088a5722aec27e.docx","sha256":"1a18ed9750e0c2d93420c8c3b756a689f6bd5ead0dcb431d42088a5722aec27e","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"Safeguarding oversight response\nMeadowgate Cooperative seasonal-worker accommodation\nPrepared for\nCamila Ellingsen, Peatmoor Networks; Ingrid Egwuatu, decision authority\nPrepared by\nPetra Yamamoto, Child and Family Social Worker\nIssued\nFriday 4 April 2025, by 16:00\nComparison scope\nEngland and Scotland | 2023–24 and 2024–25 financial years\nPosition\nLegal and practice position current as at 31 March 2025\nPurpose. Peatmoor seeks oversight following its reported increase in safeguarding referrals. This response supports Ingrid Egwuatu's enhanced family-support decision; it is not a completed child-protection assessment or an individual child assessment. The absence of the referral log, case information, occupancy/exposure data, counts, definitions, and underlying analysis means no completed quantitative comparison or case assessment can safely be made this week.\nDECISION RECORDED\nStatus changed from routine monitoring to interim enhanced review. Effective 4 April 2025. Operational owner: Petra Yamamoto. Formal review point: 25 April 2025, chaired by Ingrid Egwuatu.\nRecommendation to Ingrid: begin a proportionate, time-limited enhanced family-support response now. Continue, adjust, or close it only at the dated evidence review; this is precautionary and is not a finding that harm has increased.\n1. What the period comparison can say\nFixed scope. 2023–24 means 1 April 2023 to 31 March 2024; 2024–25 means 1 April 2024 to 31 March 2025. Both are complete, like-for-like financial years across England and Scotland.\nTerm\nRequired use in this response and later comparison\nInternally recorded safeguarding concern\nA concern recorded within Meadowgate. It is neither an allegation proved nor a statutory decision.\nOnward statutory referral\nA matter passed to local authority social work/children's social care, police, or another statutory service. It is not an assessment outcome or substantiated finding.\nPeatmoor's “referral”\nA reported umbrella term only until its components are verified. Do not combine internal concerns and onward statutory referrals into one metric; report the two series separately.\nStage discipline\nConcern received → internal screening → onward statutory referral/consultation where indicated → statutory assessment → substantiated finding. These stages are not interchangeable.\nDefensible conclusion. The only trend statement available is that Peatmoor reported an increase. Its magnitude, rate, composition, location, response route, and significance remain unverified. It does not demonstrate increased harm, abuse, employer culpability, or any substantiated safeguarding finding.\n• Measures to verify: report absolute event counts separately for internal concerns and onward statutory referrals; report unique children and unique families separately to show reach. Link true duplicate records and exclude them from totals with an audit trail; retain and identify genuine repeat episodes rather than erasing them.\n• Exposure and comparability: alongside counts, calculate a rate only against a verified denominator such as resident children, occupied family units, or family-months. Reconcile consistent definitions, reporting cut-offs, completeness, missing fields, record dates, and any change in recording practice before comparing periods.\n• Breakdown and interpretation: where safe, separate England/Scotland, safely grouped location, concern type, response route, and agency response/outcome field. Test changed occupancy, awareness, access to reporting, recording practice, category mix, duplicates, and repeat contacts as hypotheses, not findings.\n2. Safeguarding boundaries and present-safety rule\nEngland\nScotland\nSection 17 Children Act 1989 support for a child in need may be appropriate without proving abuse or reaching a section 47 threshold. Under section 47, the relevant local authority acts where it has reasonable cause to suspect a child is suffering, or is likely to suffer, significant harm. Meadowgate does not make statutory enquiries or findings. Route ordinary concerns to the relevant local authority children's social care service under local procedures; police or emergency services are for immediate danger, urgent medical need, or suspected crime, not every welfare concern.\nSection 22 Children (Scotland) Act 1995 describes the local authority support role for a child in need; receipt of support is not a child-protection determination. Information that a child may be at risk of significant harm goes promptly to local social work or police. Statutory partners decide whether an inter-agency referral discussion or other action is required. Referral to the Principal Reporter or compulsory measures is not an automatic consequence of each concern.\nCross-border rule. For urgent action, contact services where the child is currently present. Coordinate with any home or ordinarily responsible authority afterwards as needed; Meadowgate's headquarters, a responsibility discussion, or a local reporting cut-off must not delay safety action. Use the applicable local safeguarding procedures and consultation routes. Meadowgate may screen, support, record, consult, and refer, but local thresholds do not transfer statutory decision-making powers to Meadowgate.\nNo-delay rule. Data collection, management review, consent-seeking, and the 16:00 oversight deadline never delay action on a current protection concern. Immediate danger, urgent medical need, a missing child, continuing access by a suspected perpetrator, or inability to secure safety requires immediate protective or emergency action.\nDisposition\nOperational boundary\nImmediate escalation\nScreen each known matter and every live disclosure for current child location, immediate safety, basic needs, urgent medical need, alleged-perpetrator access, missing-child concerns, and whether statutory services are already involved. Escalate/consult without waiting for substantiation where there is reasonable concern about significant harm, immediate danger, urgent unmet need, criminal conduct, or inability to secure safety.\nRecord and monitored support\nOnly lower-level welfare concerns with no current significant-harm or immediate-danger indicator after screening may be recorded and monitored. Petra Yamamoto sets a safe contact/observation plan and a reconsideration point no later than 25 April 2025; repeat, cumulative, or changed concerns trigger re-screening and escalation consideration. Proportionate support may still begin.\n3. Family contact, information, and privacy controls\n• Participation and access: seek the child's views and give them due weight in a way suited to age, understanding, communication, and safety; the child does not carry the safeguarding decision. Involve parents/carers and seek consent where safe and appropriate. Consent is not a veto where seeking it may increase risk or impede necessary safeguarding action; record the rationale for delayed involvement or sharing without consent.\n• Safe communication: arrange accessible contact and an independent competent interpreter where needed. Do not use a child, another resident, or an employer representative to interpret sensitive safeguarding disclosures. Check whether an employer, accommodation controller, family member, or alleged perpetrator may monitor access; use a safe direct channel or agreed alternative where necessary.\n• Seasonal-worker context: consider language, unfamiliarity with services, employment or accommodation dependency, isolation, and fear of retaliation as possible barriers to help-seeking. They inform a tailored support offer and do not, by themselves, evidence harm.\n• Information sharing: share information lawfully when necessary and proportionate for safeguarding, using an appropriate data-protection basis and the minimum necessary disclosure. Privacy is protected but absolute confidentiality cannot be promised. Record contemporaneously what was shared or withheld, with whom, why, under whose decision, and when.\n• Oversight reporting: send Camila aggregated or appropriately suppressed information only. Do not show a location cell, or combination of location, jurisdiction, type, and route, that could identify a family at a one-family or similarly small location. Combine categories or suppress the result; any necessary case-level review uses a secure, controlled-access, need-to-know route rather than routine transmission.\n4. Accountable action schedule\nAction\nOwner\nTiming\nObservable result\nStatus control\nPetra Yamamoto\nIn force 4 April 2025; formal review 25 April 2025\nInterim enhanced review recorded and operated; Ingrid Egwuatu chairs the dated decision review.\nCase-by-case present-safety triage\nPetra Yamamoto\nBy 8 April 2025, 12:00; immediately for a live urgent matter\nScreen record for every known/live matter, safe-contact plan, and immediate jurisdiction-appropriate escalation where indicated; urgent matters do not wait for the batch review.\nEvidence retrieval and reconciliation\nCamila Ellingsen\nBy 15 April 2025\nControlled provision of pseudonymised referral records, definitions, period cut-offs, response/outcome fields where known, duplicates/repeats treatment, and verified occupancy/exposure denominators; reproducible separate-series comparison.\nFamily support and statutory coordination\nPetra Yamamoto\nImmediate for threshold concerns; by 10 April 2025 for safe needs-led support offers\nCurrent-location consultation/referral through local procedure where indicated; accessible, non-coercive family contact and independent interpreter arrangement where needed. Statutory agencies retain their decisions.\nOversight update\nPetra Yamamoto to Camila Ellingsen and Ingrid Egwuatu\n23 April 2025\nSuppressed/aggregated verified comparison, data-quality limitations, action status, jurisdictional breakdowns where safe, and evidence for Ingrid's 25 April 2025 decision.\nDecision gate\nIngrid Egwuatu\n25 April 2025\nUse the update, current-safety dispositions, support uptake where known, and verified data limits to continue, adjust, or close the enhanced family-support response.\n5. Recommendation to Ingrid Egwuatu\nApprove and maintain the interim enhanced review effective 4 April 2025, with Petra Yamamoto as operational owner and a 25 April 2025 review chaired by Ingrid Egwuatu. Begin the proportionate, time-limited enhanced family-support response now, alongside threshold-based escalation where required. At the review, decide whether to continue, adjust, or close it using the verified comparison, data-quality limits, and action evidence. It is precautionary, not a finding that harm has increased, and does not replace statutory action.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The required top-level file exists under the exact filename meadowgate_safeguarding_response.docx and is a valid, openable DOCX.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted file and DOCX package structure.","grader_hint":"Confirm the exact case-sensitive filename at the working-directory root and verify that the DOCX opens without repair or corruption."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The complete rendered response is no more than six pages, including tables and appendices.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX.","grader_hint":"Render with standard office software and count all pages. This is a hard cap with no tolerance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document is concise, legible, and written as an oversight response rather than a family-facing note, academic essay, or generic safeguarding policy.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Tone, organization, and rendered presentation throughout the document.","grader_hint":"Judge whether Camila can identify the conclusion, evidential limits, governing boundaries, status, and accountable actions without reconstructing them from dense prose."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response identifies Peatmoor’s oversight purpose and frames the work around Ingrid’s enhanced family-support decision rather than treating the document as a completed child-protection assessment.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Opening, purpose statement, and conclusion.","grader_hint":"Look for a direct connection between the response, Peatmoor’s review, and the decision Ingrid must make."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states that its legal and practice position is current as at 31 March 2025.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Date or scope statement in the document.","grader_hint":"Accept an equivalent unambiguous formulation containing 31 March 2025; a document creation date alone does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The comparison scope expressly covers England and Scotland and the 2023–24 and 2024–25 financial years.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Scope statement or comparison section.","grader_hint":"All four scope elements must appear and must be treated as the boundaries of the response."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The response fixes consistent period boundaries for both financial years, with like-for-like start and end dates or an explicit note that the final period is complete only through 31 March 2025.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Period definitions and any comparison framework.","grader_hint":"Verify that the two periods use the same financial-year convention and that no partial-period figure is implicitly compared with a full year."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Before relying on the word “referral,” the response pins down whether it means an internally recorded safeguarding concern, an onward contact with statutory services, or a clearly stated umbrella term covering separately reported categories.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Definition, terminology note, or comparison key.","grader_hint":"The ambiguity must be resolved explicitly; merely repeating Peatmoor’s word is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chosen referral definition is used consistently, and internal concerns and onward statutory referrals are not combined into one metric unless each component remains separately visible.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Terminology and all references to comparison measures.","grader_hint":"Trace each use of referral through the document and confirm that the unit does not silently change between narrative, table, conclusion, and actions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response identifies the missing referral records, case information, exposure or occupancy data, and numerical basis as material limitations preventing a completed quantitative comparison or case assessment.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Evidence limitations statement.","grader_hint":"The limitations should explain their effect on the conclusion, not appear as a generic disclaimer."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The period conclusion is limited to the fact that an increase was reported and states that its magnitude, rate, composition, and significance remain unverified.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Comparison conclusion and executive summary.","grader_hint":"Check that every summary of the trend preserves the distinction between a reported direction and a verified quantitative result."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The proposed comparison distinguishes event counts from counts of unique children or families and identifies which unit would answer each question.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Comparison specification or data requirements.","grader_hint":"Credit a defensible equivalent that prevents one family with repeated contacts from being mistaken for several affected families."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response requires duplicate records and repeat concerns to be identified and handled transparently rather than either recounting duplicates or erasing repeat episodes.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Data reconciliation or comparison requirements.","grader_hint":"Look for separate treatment of true duplicates and legitimately repeated events."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The proposed comparison includes an exposure-sensitive rate using an appropriate verified denominator, alongside absolute counts, so a change in seasonal population is not mistaken for a change in risk.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Comparison measures or required data.","grader_hint":"Accept occupied family units, resident children, family-months, or another justified exposure measure; no numeric result is expected."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response calls for comparable breakdowns that can distinguish jurisdiction, accommodation location or grouping, concern type, and response route without presenting unlike categories as equivalent.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Comparison framework or reporting plan.","grader_hint":"The exact format may vary, but the dimensions must support analysis of where the reported change occurred and how matters were handled."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The data-verification approach addresses consistent definitions, reporting cutoffs, completeness, missing fields, and changes in recording practice between periods.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Verification criteria or action plan.","grader_hint":"Credit only if the response recognizes that a clean-looking total can still be non-comparable because collection practice changed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response identifies plausible explanations other than increased harm, such as changed occupancy, awareness, access to reporting, recording practice, or repeat contacts, and treats them as hypotheses to test rather than findings.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Interpretation of the reported increase.","grader_hint":"Look for balanced alternatives and explicit uncertainty; speculative certainty in either direction does not earn credit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"For England, the response accurately distinguishes support for a child in need under section 17 of the Children Act 1989 from a finding that abuse occurred or that the section 47 threshold is met.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"England legal and practice boundary.","grader_hint":"The document need not quote the statute, but it must convey that section 17 support can be appropriate without substantiated maltreatment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"For England, the response states the section 47 boundary as reasonable cause to suspect that a child is suffering or is likely to suffer significant harm, while leaving statutory enquiries and findings to the responsible agencies.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"England escalation discussion.","grader_hint":"Do not credit wording that requires proof before referral or suggests Meadowgate itself can make a statutory finding."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"For England, concerns are routed to the relevant local authority children’s social care service, with police or emergency services used for immediate danger or suspected crime rather than automatically for every welfare concern.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"England reporting route and urgent-action guidance.","grader_hint":"Both the ordinary route and the narrower emergency or police boundary must be clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"For Scotland, the response recognizes the local authority’s section 22 Children (Scotland) Act 1995 support role for children in need and does not equate receipt of support with a child-protection determination.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Scotland support boundary.","grader_hint":"Accept accurate plain-language treatment of section 22; the key distinction is supportive need versus a proven protection concern."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"For Scotland, information indicating that a child may be at risk of significant harm is directed promptly to local social work or police, with any inter-agency referral discussion treated as a statutory-agency decision rather than an automatic result of every concern.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Scotland child-protection escalation discussion.","grader_hint":"Look for correct agency roles and a threshold-based approach without requiring the worker to establish harm first."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The Scotland discussion distinguishes a safeguarding or child-protection concern from referral to the Principal Reporter and compulsory measures, which are not automatic consequences of each concern.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Scotland pathway explanation.","grader_hint":"Credit an accurate equivalent even if the Children’s Hearings terminology is brief."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"For a cross-border case, the response bases urgent contact on where the child is currently present and coordinates with any home or ordinarily responsible authority as needed, rather than selecting a route by Meadowgate’s headquarters.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Cross-jurisdiction reporting rule.","grader_hint":"The document should prevent delay caused by debating corporate location or longer-term responsibility before addressing current safety."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response requires use of applicable local safeguarding procedures and consultation routes while making clear that local thresholds do not transfer statutory decision-making powers to Meadowgate.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Cross-jurisdiction qualifications or action plan.","grader_hint":"Look for respect for local procedural variation without inventing a single UK-wide statutory process."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Immediate danger, urgent medical need, a missing child, or continuing access by a suspected perpetrator triggers immediate protective or emergency action without waiting for the full period comparison.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Urgent escalation rule.","grader_hint":"Equivalent urgent indicators are acceptable, but the response must make clear that data verification does not delay present-safety action."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response treats concern received, internal screening, onward referral, statutory assessment, and substantiated finding as distinct stages rather than interchangeable labels.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Terminology and escalation framework.","grader_hint":"Check that the distinctions are understandable to an oversight reader and remain consistent throughout."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Family support is presented as capable of starting on a proportionate, voluntary or needs-led basis without waiting for proof of abuse, but not as a substitute for statutory escalation where the protection threshold may be met.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Family-support recommendation and safeguarding boundaries.","grader_hint":"Both sides of the boundary must appear: support need not await substantiation, and support cannot replace protective reporting."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response explicitly prevents data collection, management review, consent-seeking, or the oversight deadline from delaying action on a current protection concern.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Escalation rule and action sequence.","grader_hint":"Credit a clear priority rule rather than a general statement that safeguarding is important."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Parents or carers are to be involved and consent sought where safe and appropriate, but consent is not presented as an absolute veto where seeking it could increase risk or impede necessary safeguarding action.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Consent and family-involvement discussion.","grader_hint":"The response should require a recorded rationale when involvement is delayed or information is shared without consent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The child’s views are to be sought and given due weight in an age-, understanding-, communication-, and safety-appropriate manner without making the child responsible for the safeguarding decision.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Assessment or contact approach.","grader_hint":"Look for both participation and the limit on transferring professional responsibility to the child."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Information sharing is framed as lawful where necessary and proportionate for safeguarding, subject to an appropriate data-protection basis and minimum-necessary disclosure; data protection is neither treated as a blanket barrier nor as permission for unrestricted sharing.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Information-sharing rule.","grader_hint":"A precise article citation is not required, but the two-sided boundary must be legally and professionally sound."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response requires a contemporaneous record of what was shared or withheld, with whom, why, under whose decision, and when.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Recordkeeping requirement or action plan.","grader_hint":"Credit a clear audit trail requirement; a generic instruction to keep records is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response avoids promising absolute confidentiality and instead explains that privacy will be protected subject to necessary safeguarding disclosure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Contact, consent, or confidentiality language.","grader_hint":"Look for wording suitable for oversight review that would not create an unsafe promise to a child or family."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response provides for accessible communication and an independent competent interpreter where needed, without relying on a child, another resident, or an employer representative to interpret sensitive safeguarding disclosures.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Contact and support arrangements.","grader_hint":"Both accessibility and interpreter independence should be apparent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The seasonal-worker context is addressed through relevant factors such as language, unfamiliarity with services, employment or accommodation dependency, isolation, and fear of retaliation, without treating seasonal-worker status itself as evidence of harm.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Contextual risk analysis and support plan.","grader_hint":"Credit a tailored, non-stereotyping analysis rather than a generic vulnerability label."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Contact arrangements account for whether an employer, accommodation controller, family member, or alleged perpetrator could monitor access or increase risk, and provide a safe alternative channel where necessary.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Safe-contact arrangements.","grader_hint":"Look for an executable privacy and safety precaution, not merely a direction to contact the family."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The immediate review screens each known matter for current child location, immediate safety, basic needs, medical urgency, alleged perpetrator access, missing-child concerns, and whether statutory services are already involved.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Triage criteria or action plan.","grader_hint":"Equivalent concise criteria are acceptable if they support a safe present-risk decision without attempting a full assessment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The escalation boundary covers information giving reasonable concern about significant harm, immediate danger, urgent unmet need, criminal conduct, or inability to secure safety, and does not require substantiation before consultation or referral.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Escalation criteria.","grader_hint":"Assess whether a worker could identify matters that must leave the monitor-only route."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The record-and-monitor boundary is limited to lower-level welfare concerns with no current significant-harm or immediate-danger indicator after screening, while still permitting proportionate support.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Monitoring criteria.","grader_hint":"The monitor route must be a reasoned safeguarding disposition, not a default caused by missing records."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Monitoring is time-limited and includes a named owner, review date, contact or observation plan, escalation triggers, and a requirement to reconsider cumulative or repeat concerns.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Monitoring controls and action plan.","grader_hint":"All elements should be concrete enough to prevent indefinite passive monitoring."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Given the reported increase and missing case pack, the response adopts a time-limited interim enhanced review as the safe fallback while explicitly avoiding a finding that harm has increased.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Status decision and rationale.","grader_hint":"The fallback should increase scrutiny and support pending verification, not classify all matters as child-protection cases or postpone current-risk screening."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The authorized status change is carried out in the document and records interim enhanced review, an effective date, a named owner, and a definite review point that agree everywhere they appear.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Status statement, action table, and conclusion.","grader_hint":"A recommendation that someone else change the status later is insufficient. Cross-check all repeated dates, owners, and status labels for consistency."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Ingrid receives one unambiguous recommendation on enhanced family-support action: a proportionate, time-limited enhanced response should begin now, with its continuation or adjustment tied to the scheduled evidence review.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Recommendation and conclusion.","grader_hint":"The recommendation may be qualified by safeguards and a review gate, but it must not offer routine monitoring and enhanced action as equal unresolved options."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A named owner and near-term deadline are assigned for case-by-case safety triage, with urgent matters escalated immediately rather than held for the batch review.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Action plan.","grader_hint":"The owner and timing must be executable and must distinguish urgent handling from the general deadline."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A named owner and deadline are assigned to obtain and reconcile the referral records, denominator data, definitions, duplicates, repeat episodes, and outcome or response fields needed for the verified comparison.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Data and reporting action.","grader_hint":"The action must lead to a reproducible comparison rather than merely asking for more information."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A named owner and timing are assigned for jurisdiction-appropriate consultation, referrals, and family-support contact, with responsibility divided clearly enough to avoid diffusion between Meadowgate and statutory agencies.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Safeguarding action plan.","grader_hint":"Check that operational ownership is clear while statutory decisions remain with the proper agencies."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response resolves the audit-detail versus re-identification tension by using aggregated or appropriately suppressed oversight reporting and a controlled route for any necessary case-level review.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Reporting design and privacy controls.","grader_hint":"Accept a defensible suppression, category-combination, or secure-review approach with an explicit rule; simply removing names is insufficient for one-family locations."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document commits to a dated oversight update containing the verified comparison, data-quality limitations, action status, jurisdictional breakdowns where safe, and the evidence Ingrid will use at the review point.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Reporting action and decision gate.","grader_hint":"The update must have an owner or accountable party, a definite date, and specified content rather than being an open-ended promise to report back."},{"rubric_item_id":"P01","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response invents or presents as known any referral count, rate, occupancy value, case event, outcome, demographic fact, or location result that was not supplied.","weight":-8.0,"polarity":"penalty","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"All factual and quantitative assertions in the document.","grader_hint":"Apply once if any unsupported case-specific or numerical result is asserted. Clearly labelled blank fields, proposed measures, or hypothetical examples that cannot be mistaken for case facts do not trigger the penalty."},{"rubric_item_id":"P02","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response states or implies that the reported increase, an internal concern, or an onward referral proves abuse, significant harm, employer culpability, or a substantiated safeguarding finding.","weight":-8.0,"polarity":"penalty","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Executive summary, comparison interpretation, legal analysis, and recommendation.","grader_hint":"Apply once for an affirmative unsupported finding or equivalent wording. Do not apply merely because the document recommends precautionary enhanced support."},{"rubric_item_id":"P03","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response includes, requests for routine transmission, or proposes unsuppressed reporting of information that could identify a child or family at a one-family or similarly small location when aggregate or controlled review would meet the oversight need.","weight":-8.0,"polarity":"penalty","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Tables, examples, requested data fields, appendices, and reporting instructions.","grader_hint":"Apply once for actual identifying detail or a reporting design that predictably exposes a family through small cells or combinations of attributes."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"Meadowgate Cooperative operates seasonal-worker accommodation in England and Scotland. Peatmoor Networks is reviewing an unexplained reported rise in safeguarding referrals and needs a response that supports an immediate family-support decision without overstating what the unavailable case evidence proves.","difficulty_sources":["Resolving an audience’s ambiguous use of referral before comparing periods.","Applying distinct England and Scotland safeguarding boundaries without a case pack.","Separating precautionary support and monitoring from matters requiring statutory escalation.","Producing a defensible response without inventing quantitative or case-level evidence.","Balancing oversight transparency against re-identification risk at one-family locations.","Taking and recording an authorized interim action under a short operational deadline."],"authoring_plan":{"reference_material":{"files_to_create":[],"reason":"The task intentionally supplies no case pack, referral log, denominator data, or policy extract. Solvability rests on occupational knowledge and explicit treatment of evidential limits."},"required_imperfections":["The only trend information is Peatmoor’s unsupported directional statement that referrals increased.","There are no counts, rates, case records, occupancy measures, outcomes, or data definitions.","The term referral is deliberately ambiguous between an internal concern and an onward statutory referral.","England and Scotland require related but non-identical statutory language and pathways.","Location-level oversight detail creates a re-identification risk because some locations had only one family.","The operational deadline precedes any feasible full assessment."],"gold_reasoning":{"comparison_rule":"State only that an increase was reported. Do not calculate or infer a magnitude. A future verified comparison must use fixed financial-year boundaries, separately defined internal concerns and onward referrals, unique event and unique child or family measures, transparent duplicate and repeat-event handling, absolute counts, and a verified exposure denominator.","interpretation_rule":"Do not infer increased harm from reporting volume. Test occupancy, awareness, reporting access, recording changes, category mix, duplicates, repeat contacts, jurisdiction, and outcomes before interpreting the trend.","england_rule":"Distinguish Children Act 1989 section 17 support from section 47 reasonable-cause-to-suspect-significant-harm enquiries. Route non-emergency safeguarding concerns to the relevant local authority children’s social care service; use police or emergency services for immediate danger or suspected crime. Meadowgate may screen, support, record, and refer but may not make a statutory finding.","scotland_rule":"Distinguish section 22 Children (Scotland) Act 1995 support from child-protection action. Where information indicates possible significant harm, contact local social work or police promptly; statutory partners determine whether an inter-agency referral discussion or other action is required. Do not present referral to the Principal Reporter or compulsory measures as automatic.","cross_border_rule":"Address immediate risk through services where the child is currently present and coordinate with any home or ordinarily responsible authority without allowing responsibility disputes to delay safety action.","consent_and_information_rule":"Involve parents or carers and seek consent when safe and appropriate, but do not allow consent to block necessary safeguarding action. Share information on a necessary, proportionate, minimum-necessary basis using an appropriate lawful basis, record the decision, and do not promise absolute confidentiality.","support_rule":"Needs-led family support can begin without proof of abuse and should address communication, access, employment or accommodation dependency, isolation, and retaliation concerns. It cannot replace statutory escalation where significant harm may be present.","triage_rule":"Each known matter must receive prompt present-safety screening. Immediate danger, urgent medical need, a missing child, continuing alleged-perpetrator access, inability to meet basic needs, or credible significant-harm information leaves the monitor-only route.","monitoring_rule":"A lower-level concern may be recorded and monitored only after current-risk screening shows no immediate-danger or significant-harm indicator. Monitoring must be time-limited, owned, reviewed, and sensitive to cumulative or repeat concerns.","privacy_rule":"Give Peatmoor aggregate or suppressed reporting with an explicit small-cell protection rule. Any necessary case-level verification occurs through a controlled-access route rather than the ordinary oversight response."},"intended_status_and_verdict":{"authorized_status":"Change the matter from routine monitoring to interim enhanced review in the response itself.","status_rationale":"The unexplained reported increase and absent records justify increased scrutiny and proportionate support, but not a finding that harm increased or that every matter requires statutory escalation.","longer_term_recommendation":"Recommend a time-limited enhanced family-support response beginning now, with continuation, adjustment, or closure decided at a scheduled review after case triage and data reconciliation.","required_status_record":"Include an effective date, a named owner selected from the permitted people, and a definite review date. These details must agree throughout the document."},"action_specification":["Assign prompt case-by-case safety triage, with immediate escalation outside the batch timetable where required.","Assign retrieval and reconciliation of pseudonymized referral records and occupancy or exposure data.","Assign safe, accessible family contact and interpreter provision where required.","Assign jurisdiction-specific consultation or referral using the child’s current location.","Set a dated review gate for Ingrid Egwuatu and a dated oversight update for Camila Ellingsen.","Ensure every action has a named owner, concrete timing, and an observable completion result."],"vocabulary_and_conventions":{"solver_visible_source":"The request itself carries the local status options routine monitoring and interim enhanced review, the exact filename, audience names, jurisdictions, periods, deadline, and as-of date.","professional_terms_expected":["internally recorded safeguarding concern","onward statutory referral","child in need","significant harm","section 17","section 47","section 22","inter-agency referral discussion","necessary and proportionate information sharing","interim enhanced review"],"no_private_codes":"Do not introduce an unstated local code, mandatory table heading, suppression number, or agency-specific form name into the gold or rubric."},"space_feasibility":"A six-page DOCX permits a concise decision summary, evidence limits and comparison framework, England and Scotland boundaries, triage and monitoring rules, privacy treatment, status decision, and accountable action schedule. The gold should remain materially below the cap when rendered with normal professional formatting.","offline_validation":"An imagined reference response can be authored with python-docx, rendered through LibreOffice, inspected for page count and consistency, and reviewed against every rubric item without internet access or external files. No rubric item requires unavailable case facts."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"mixed_media_bundle","structure":"required_sections"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"customer_case_response","filename":"meadowgate_safeguarding_response.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"An oversight-ready safeguarding case response comparing the two reported periods, applying the England and Scotland boundaries, recording the authorized interim status, and supporting the enhanced family-support decision.","required":true,"constraints":["Place the file at the top level of the working directory.","Use exactly the filename meadowgate_safeguarding_response.docx.","Maximum six rendered pages, including tables and appendices.","Apply law and practice as at 31 March 2025.","Do not invent quantitative results, case outcomes, incidents, or identifying details."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"customer_case_response","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"child_family_school_social_workers","sector":"government","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":4.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"The reference response must be reviewed independently by one practitioner experienced in child and family safeguarding and one reviewer competent in the England–Scotland statutory and information-sharing distinctions. Reviewers must verify the status decision, legal boundaries, privacy treatment, and consistency of owners and dates in the rendered document.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No case-level or quantitative source data exist, so the gold cannot contain a verified numerical comparison.","Local authority procedures vary; the gold should state national statutory and practice boundaries and require local-procedure confirmation without inventing council-specific rules.","The artifact supports immediate triage, interim support, and reporting decisions but is not a substitute for an individual child assessment."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:59:23+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:59:23+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:59:23+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:59:23+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/dac213f38c294e77","declared_deliverables":["meadowgate_safeguarding_response.docx"]};
